<article class="env-lemma" id="08CE">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08CE">Lemma <span data-tag="08CE">20.47.6</span>.</a> Let $(X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$ be a ringed space. Let $m \in \mathbf{Z}$. If $K \oplus L$ is $m$-pseudo-coherent (resp. pseudo-coherent) in $D(\mathcal{O}_ X)$ so are $K$ and $L$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume that $K \oplus L$ is $m$-pseudo-coherent. After replacing $X$ by the members of an open covering we may assume $K \oplus L \in D^-(\mathcal{O}_ X)$, hence $L \in D^-(\mathcal{O}_ X)$. Note that there is a distinguished triangle 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  (K \oplus L, K \oplus L, L \oplus L[1]) = (K, K, 0) \oplus (L, L, L \oplus L[1])  \]
</div>
    <p>
       see Derived Categories, Lemma <a href="/tag/05QS" data-tag="05QS">13.4.10</a>. By Lemma <a href="/tag/08CD" data-tag="08CD">20.47.4</a> we see that $L \oplus L[1]$ is $m$-pseudo-coherent. Hence also $L[1] \oplus L[2]$ is $m$-pseudo-coherent. By induction $L[n] \oplus L[n + 1]$ is $m$-pseudo-coherent. Since $L$ is bounded above we see that $L[n]$ is $m$-pseudo-coherent for large $n$. Hence working backwards, using the distinguished triangles 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  (L[n], L[n] \oplus L[n - 1], L[n - 1])  \]
</div>
    <p>
       we conclude that $L[n - 1], L[n - 2], \ldots , L$ are $m$-pseudo-coherent as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>